=== layout: one-panel
# Mendel's Four Principles of Heredity

@body

- [*Paired factors*]{underline=true}: diploid organisms have two alleles at a locus.
- [*Dominance*]{underline=true}: in complete dominance, the dominant allele determines the heterozygote phenotype.
- [*Segregation*]{underline=true}: each gamete receives one allele of each pair.
- [*Independent assortment*]{underline=true}: unlinked genes assort independently.

=== layout: two-panels
# Mendel’s Principles were Imperfect

@left

![Slide image 1](assets/lect06d-x_linked_genes_1/image_001.png)

@right

- In Chapters 4 and 5, we will be discussing the contributions of Thomas Hunt Morgan.
- Morgan brought Mendel’s ideas to molecular biology
- in doing so Morgan expanded on and falsified Mendel original ideas.

=== layout: one-panel
# Mendel's Four Principles of Heredity

@body

- [*Paired factors*]{underline=true}: diploid organisms have two alleles at a locus.
- [*Dominance*]{underline=true}: in complete dominance, the dominant allele determines the heterozygote phenotype.
- [*Segregation*]{underline=true}: each gamete receives one allele of each pair.
- [*Independent assortment*]{underline=true}: unlinked genes assort independently.

- Exceptions: [NOT ALL ORGANISMS ARE DIPLOID]{color=#CC00CC}; dominance can be [INCOMPLETE or CODOMINANT]{color=#CC00CC}; genes can interact ([EPISTASIS]{color=#CC00CC}).

=== layout: two-panels
# Morgan showed that inheritance has a physical basis

@left

- The first solid evidence associating a specific gene with a specific chromosome came in the early 20th century from the work of [Thomas Hunt Morgan]{color=blue}
- These early experiments provided convincing evidence that the chromosomes are the location of Mendel’s heritable factors

@right

![Slide image 2](assets/lect06d-x_linked_genes_1/image_002.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# Morgan’s Choice of Organism

@left

- Morgan studied the fruit fly, *Drosophila melanogaster*.
- Females lay about 400 eggs.
- A new generation every two weeks.
- Only four pairs of chromosomes.

@right

![Slide image 3](assets/lect06d-x_linked_genes_1/image_003.png)

=== layout: one-panel
# Drosophila chromosomes

@body

![Slide image 4](assets/lect06d-x_linked_genes_1/image_004.png)

=== layout: one-panel
# Wild Type

@body

- Morgan noted [*wild type*]{color=red underline=true}, or normal, phenotypes that were common in the fly populations
  - [*wild type*]{color=red underline=true} literally means the most common phenotype
- Traits alternative to the [*wild type*]{color=red underline=true} are called [*mutant*]{color=blue underline=true} phenotypes

=== layout: one-panel

@body

![Slide image 5](assets/lect06d-x_linked_genes_1/image_005.jpg)

- Wild-type Drosophila flies have red eyes (left).
- Among his flies, Morgan discovered a mutant male with white eyes (right).
- This variation made it possible for Morgan to trace a gene for eye color to a specific chromosome (LMs).

=== layout: one-panel
# Correlating Behavior of a Gene’s Alleles with Behavior of a Chromosome Pair

@body

- In one experiment, Morgan mated male flies with white eyes (mutant) with female flies with red eyes (wild type)
  - The F1 generation all had red eyes
  - The F2 generation showed a 3\:1 red to white eye ratio, but [only males had white eyes]{color=blue}
- Morgan determined that the white-eyed mutant allele must be located on the X chromosome
- Morgan’s finding supported the chromosome theory of inheritance

