=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

Duchenne muscular dystrophy is caused by an X-linked recessive allele. How likely is a woman to have this condition?

- A) Women can never have this condition.
- B) One-fourth of an affected man's daughters would have it.
- C) Half of an affected father and carrier mother's daughters could have it.
- D) Only women with three X chromosomes could have it.

@answer

Answer: C

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

A man with _achondroplasia and normal vision_ marries a _color-blind woman of normal height._ His father and both of her parents have normal height. Achondroplasia is autosomal dominant; color blindness is X-linked recessive. How many daughters would be color-blind and have achondroplasia?

- A) None
- B) One out of four
- C) Half
- D) Three out of four
- E) All

@answer

Answer: A

=== layout: multiple-choice
@question

A man who is an achondroplastic dwarf with normal vision marries a color-blind woman of normal height.
The man's father was six feet tall, and both the woman's parents were of average height.
Achondroplastic dwarfism is autosomal dominant, and red-green color blindness is X- linked recessive.
What proportion of their sons would be color-blind and of normal height?

- A) none
- B) one out of four
- C) half
- D) three out of four
- E) all

@answer

[Answer\: C]{color=red}

=== layout: multiple-choice
@question

A man who is an achondroplastic dwarf with color-blind vision marries a woman of average height and wild-type vision.
The man's father was six feet tall, and both the woman's parents were of average height. The woman’s father was also color-blind.
Achondroplastic dwarfism is autosomal dominant, and red-green color blindness is X-linked recessive.
What proportion of their daughters would be color-blind and of average height?

- A) none
- B) one out of four
- C) half
- D) three out of four
- E) all

@answer

[Answer\: B]{color=red}

